checks.expect:
Panel text: {text}
Scene layout: {layout}
From the text and the layout only (there is no picture), where should each of these people be looking: {names}? Use "left" (toward the left side of the picture), "right" (toward the right side), "viewer" (at the viewer, eye contact with the camera), "down", "up", "away" (back turned to the viewer), or "unknown" when the text does not make it clear. A person the text has talking to or looking at someone off-screen on the left looks "left".
Answer JSON only: {{"<name>": "..."}}

checks.observe:
People in this webtoon panel: {looks}
For each of them ({names}), where do their face and eyes point, as you look at the picture? Use "left" (toward the left side of the picture), "right" (toward the right side), "viewer" (at the viewer, eye contact with the camera), "down", "up", "away" (back turned to the viewer), or "unclear" if you cannot tell who is who.
Answer JSON only: {{"<name>": "..."}}

checks.ask:
Panel text: {text}
Is any person in this webtoon panel looking straight at the viewer (eye contact with the camera) while the panel text does not ask for that? People normally look at the person or thing they are dealing with, like a candid film still.
Report only where faces, eyes and shoulders point; objects, props and where things are placed are checked elsewhere, do not list them.
{common}
Answer JSON only: {{"problems": ["..."]}} (empty list if none).

Panel text: {text}
For each person, look at the direction of their FACE and SHOULDERS, not only the eyes. Compare with the panel text: people who deal with each other turn toward each other (profile or three-quarter view) unless the text asks for something else. A person whose shoulders and face are square to the viewer when the text does not ask for it is a mismatch, even if the eyes glance sideways. List only clear mismatches, naming each person by clothes or position.
Report only where faces, eyes and shoulders point; objects, props and where things are placed are checked elsewhere, do not list them.
{common}
Answer JSON only: {{"problems": ["..."]}} (empty list if none).

checks.expect:
Panel text: {text}
Scene layout: {layout}
From the text and the layout only, on which side of the picture (as the viewer sees it) should each of these people be: {names}? Answer "unknown" when the text and layout do not say.
Answer JSON only: {{"<name>": "left" | "right" | "unknown"}}

checks.observe:
People in this webtoon panel: {looks}
For each of them ({names}), is the person on the left or the right side of the picture, as you look at it? Answer "unclear" if you cannot tell who is who.
Answer JSON only: {{"<name>": "left" | "right" | "unclear"}}

checks.ask:
Panel text: {text}
Compare the camera with the panel text and report only big mismatches: a close-up drawn as a wide shot or the reverse, a person seen from the back when the text needs their face or the reverse, seats or vehicles facing the opposite way from the text.
{common}
Answer JSON only: {{"problems": ["..."]}} (empty list if none).

You are a strict script supervisor. Panel text: {text}
Check only what a careful reader would notice: something that works the wrong way in the real world, a person standing while the text has them seated or the reverse, the number or kind of key objects not matching the text, a speaking person whose face cannot be seen when the text does not ask for that.{era}
Do not report where people look or which side they are on (other checks cover that). List only clear mistakes.
{common}
Answer JSON only: {{"problems": ["..."]}} (empty list if none).

Panel text: {text}
Look only at what each person holds or uses with their hands. Is anyone holding or using an object the panel text does not mention, or missing an object the text says they hold? Things worn on the body (rings, watches, glasses, bags on a shoulder) are not held objects.
{common}
Answer JSON only: {{"problems": ["..."]}} (empty list if none).

checks.expect:
Panel text: {text}
People with a spoken line in THIS panel: {speakers}.
From the text only, what should each person's mouth do: {names}? Use "talking" (they speak aloud in this panel), "shouting", "laughing", "closed" (silent: no spoken line here and the text does not say they talk), or "unknown". Answer JSON only: {{"<name>": "..."}}

checks.ask:
Panel text: {text}
Look only at objects people hold, use or that stand near their hands: phones, cups, glasses, mugs, bottles, bags. Report clear problems: an object far too small or too large for the hand or person (a phone as small as a fingertip or as big as a book, a mug bigger than a head), an object floating in the air or at the mouth or ear with no hand holding it, two of an object where one person should hold one, an object that does not fit the period.{era}
Only sizes a reader would find plainly wrong or impossible at a glance; ignore small differences. Write each problem in one short sentence.
Answer JSON only: {{"problems": ["..."]}} (empty list if none).

checks.observe:
Image 2 is the reference portrait of {name}: it decides how {name} looks. Image 1 is a webtoon panel that should show {name}.
People in image 1: {names}. Panel text: {text}
Find {name} in image 1 (use the panel text, positions and clothes to tell who is who). Compare only what kind of being {name} is drawn as, against image 2: a human face or an animal face (fur, muzzle, whiskers), animal ears, horns, skin or fur color. Ignore clothes, pose, expression, camera angle and small drawing details.
Answer JSON only: {{"found": true|false, "same_kind": true|false, "differs": "what is different, empty if the same"}}

verify:
Panel text: {text}
Another reviewer looked at this webtoon panel and listed these problems:
{problems}
Look at the picture yourself. Keep a problem only if it is clearly true in the picture AND either contradicts something the panel text states or is an obvious real-world or period error a reader would notice at a glance. Left and right always mean the viewer's left and right side of the picture. Drop guesses, nitpicks and anything the panel text does not ask for.
Answer JSON only: {{"keep": [numbers of the problems to keep, starting at 0]}}
